    kg:textSpan "DARTS: D IFFERENTIABLE A RCHITECTURE S EARCH Hanxiao Liu CMU hanxiaol@cs.cmu.com Karen Simonyan DeepMind simonyan@google.com Yiming Yang CMU yiming@cs.cmu.edu A BSTRACT This paper addresses the scalability challenge of architecture search by formulating the task in a differentiable manner. Unlike conventional approaches of applying evolution or reinforcement learning over a discrete and non-differentiable search space, our method is based on the continuous relaxation of the architecture represen" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1806_09055_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1806.09055_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "1806.09055_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1806_09055_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "to be continuous, so that the architecture can be optimized with respect to its validation set performance by gradient descent. The data efciency of gradient-based optimization, as opposed to inefcient black-box search, allows DARTS to achieve competitive performance with the state of the art using orders of magnitude less computation resources. It also outperforms another recent efcient architecture search method, ENAS ( Pham et al. , 2018b ). Notably, DARTS is simpler than many existing approa" .

