    kg:textSpan ". The total error can be affected by the three main factors of AutoML. The larger search space expands the hypothesis space H , while the search algorithm and evaluation mainly influence the optimization procedure of the bi-level problem (1) (2) . Table 3 summarizes the evolution of decomposed errors and computation time with changes of three factors. When the search space becomes larger, the approximation error will drop due to larger hypothesis space. The optimization error and computation tim" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2007_15745_rag__chunk_006> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2007.15745_rag::chunk_006" ;
    kg:docId "2007.15745_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2007_15745_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "not), or categorical ( e.g. , type of optimizer). Therefore, hyper-parameters are classied as continuous, discrete, and categorical hyper-parameters. For continuous and discrete hyper-parameters, their domains are usually bounded 8 in practical applications [12] [23]. On the other hand, the hyper-parameter conguration space sometimes contains conditionality. A hyper-parameter may need to be used or tuned depending on the value of another hyperparameter, called a conditional hyper-parameter [10]." .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2008_08516_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2008.08516_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "2008.08516_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2008_08516_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "et al. distinguish at least three levels of automation in which AutoML systems can be categorized, these are summarized as follows: level. Search of estimators/predictors . This level refers to the task of dening / determining a function mapping inputs to outputs, for example, manually setting the weights of a linear regressor for approaching a particular task (here y i R ), or generating hard-coded classiers (e.g., based on if-then rules). level. Search of learning algorithms . Refers to the ta" .

