    kg:chunkId "ETMLP5_rag::chunk_006" ;
    kg:docId "ETMLP5_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/ETMLP5_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "Understanding and [TABLE id=ETMLP5_p4_t2] caption: Table 2: Productivity and Interpretability criteria. : only for certain models columns: col1 row: col1=Criteria Auger.AI BigML DAI Darwin JAD RM Watson Data manipulation A B B A A A Pipeline automation B B A A A B A Early stop or pause B B B B [/TABLE] [TABLE id=ETMLP5_p5_t3] caption: Table 3: Customizability and Connectivity criteria. : for RM server, not RM studio columns: col1 row: col1=Criteria Auger.AI BigML DAI Darwin JAD RM Watson Time bu" .

<http://example.org/kg/paper/2007_15745_rag> a kg:Paper ;
    kg:docId "2007.15745_rag" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1803_00684_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1803.00684_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "1803.00684_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1803_00684_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "empirical success when compared to other AutoML systems. However, by stacking models on top of each other, our search space is much larger than that of single-model AutoML systems such as TPOT or AutoSklearn. Naturally the primitives in a candidate pipeline need to be optimized as well, further compounding our problem. We tackle this issue by using a basic Evolutionary Algorithm, rather than Bayesian Optimization, to nd suitable hyperparameters. EAs have recently seen a renaissance in other elds" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1810_13306_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1810.13306_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "1810.13306_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1810_13306_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "at locating instances of objects in images [ 116 , 117 ], experience ( ) corresponds to images with its labeled bounding box, and measures the performance of object detection, such as Average Precision (AP) and mean Average Precision (mAP) [ 47 , 117 ]. Another example is molecular property prediction ( ) [ 150 , 154 ], which targets at predict the property of molecules precisely. In this application, experience ( ) corresponds to molecules with its property (label) and measures the performance " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2007_15745_rag__chunk_020> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2007.15745_rag::chunk_020" ;
    kg:docId "2007.15745_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2007_15745_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "regions where the global optimum is most likely to occur, based on the posterior distribution. BO models balance the exploration and the exploitation processes to detect the current most likely optimal regions and avoid missing better congurations in the unexplored areas [88]. The basic procedures of BO are as follows [87]: 1. Build a probabilistic surrogate model of the objective function. 2. Detect the optimal hyper-parameter values on the surrogate model. 3. Apply these hyper-parameter values" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/ETMLP5_rag__chunk_002> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "ETMLP5_rag::chunk_002" ;
    kg:docId "ETMLP5_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/ETMLP5_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "is to provide a user-centric framework for comparing AutoML services. We define a set of qualitative criteria, spanning across six categories (Estimates, Scope, Productivity, Interpretability, Customizability, and Connectivity) that highlight user-experience beyond predictive performance when selecting or evaluating AutoML services. Using this framework we evaluated seven such services, namely Auger.AI [ 2 ], BigML [ 4 ], H2Os Driverless AI [ 19 ], Darwin [ 7 ], Just Add Data Bio [ 1 ], RapidMin" .

