    kg:textSpan "n ) } and the feature vectors x n +1 , . . . , x n + m of a test dataset D test = { ( x n +1 , y n +1 ) , . . . , ( x n + m , y n + m ) } drawn from the same underlying data distribution, as well as a resource budget b and a loss metric L ( , ) , the AutoML problem is to (automatically) produce test set predictions y n +1 , . . . , y n + m . The loss of a solution y n +1 , . . . , y n + m to the AutoML problem is given by 1 m P m j =1 L ( y n + j , y n + j ) . 1 In practice, the budget b would c" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1810_13306_rag__chunk_004> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1810.13306_rag::chunk_004" ;
    kg:docId "1810.13306_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1810_13306_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "attempts to atomize (all or a part of) learning configurations and then recombine them through optimizing performance measure with experience on some classes of task . According to the definition of AutoML, we can infer that AutoML targets at addressing complex design choices and high human labor cost in classical machine learning. Through the process of atomization of learning configurations, AutoML offers a broader range of design choices in the design space compared to classical machine learn" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1802_03268_rag__chunk_012> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1802.03268_rag::chunk_012" ;
    kg:docId "1802.03268_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1802_03268_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "we do not sample multiple architectures from our trained controller, train them, and then select the best architecture on the validation data. This extra step benets NASs performance. GPU-hours by more than 50,000x compared to NAS. The third block of Table 2 presents the performances of approaches that attempt to design one more more modules and then connect them together to form the nal networks. ENAS takes 11 . 5 hours to discover the convolution cell and the reduction cell, which are visualiz" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1906_02287_rag__chunk_014> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1906.02287_rag::chunk_014" ;
    kg:docId "1906.02287_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1906_02287_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "a meta-learning approach where the meta-features of the input dataset is extracted and compared with the meta-features of the datasets that are stored in the frameworks knowledge base, populated from the results of the previous runs. The similarity search process is used to identify the similar datasets in the knowledge base, using a nearest neighbor approach, where the retrieved results are used to identify the best performing algorithms on those similar datasets in order to nominate the candid" .

